# Private words for the scrub check (scripts/countersign/scrub-check.mjs).
#
# Copy this file to .scrub-patterns in the repository root and replace the
# examples with your own: employer and customer names, private email domains,
# fragments of your own folder paths. .scrub-patterns is git-ignored (so is any
# .scrub-patterns.* variant, such as an editor's .txt copy); only this example
# file is tracked. Then run `pnpm check:scrub` and `pnpm check:scrub -- --history`
# before each push. CI never sees your file and checks the built-in rules only.
#
# Format:
# - One JavaScript regular expression per line, without /slashes/ or flags.
#   Every line is matched case-insensitively.
# - Lines starting with # are comments. Blank lines are ignored.
# - (?i) and (?i:...) are not valid in JavaScript; they are not needed either.
# - A line that is not a valid expression stops the check with exit code 2 and
#   names its line number.
# - A finding prints as path:line private:<n>, where n is the line number in
#   this file. The matched text and the expression are never printed.
# - Private words are checked in every tracked file and cannot be baselined.
# - A short word also matches inside longer words. If one of your words is
#   part of your public GitHub handle, add a negative lookahead for the rest of
#   the handle, or list a longer fragment instead.
#
# Neutral examples (replace them):
\bexamplecorp\b
[a-z]:[\\/]+users[\\/]+yourname
